<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Pho</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wat Phra Chetuphon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Pho is a royal Buddhist temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, just south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a>. Rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> and greatly enlarged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a>, it houses a 46-metre gilded reclining Buddha and the largest collection of Buddha images in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Its inscriptions on medicine, history and literature made it a kind of public university, and it remains a centre of traditional Thai massage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-pho.jpg" alt="Wat Pho" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mastertongapollo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%98%E0%B9%84%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A2%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%99.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Wat Pho is one of the oldest temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> and existed before King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> made Bangkok the capital. Its first name, Wat Photaram, refers to the monastery of the Bodhi tree at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>. The date of the old temple and its founder are not known; it was built in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> period and is said to have been built or expanded under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phetracha">Phetracha</a> (1688 to 1703). After Ayutthaya fell to the Burmese in 1767, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a> built his palace in Thonburi beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-arun">Wat Arun</a>, across the Chao Phraya river, and the proximity raised Wat Pho to a royal monastery.</p><p>In 1788 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> ordered the dilapidated temple rebuilt, and had Buddha images brought from abandoned temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, Sukhothai and elsewhere, including the remnants of a huge image from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-si-sanphet">Wat Phra Si Sanphet</a>, which were enclosed in a chedi. In 1801 the new complex was renamed Wat Phra Chetuphon Vimolmangklavas and became his main temple. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a> began to renovate and enlarge it in 1832, a work of 16 years and seven months, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Rama IV</a> added a fourth great chedi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The complex has two walled compounds divided by Chetuphon Road. The northern one, the phutthawat, contains the buildings dedicated to the Buddha, and the southern one, the sankhawat, holds the monks' quarters and a school. The grounds hold four great chedis of 42 metres, dedicated to the first four Chakri kings, 91 small chedis, a bot dedicated in 1791 and about 400 Buddha images in a double cloister, chosen from 1,200 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> had brought.</p><p>The Buddha in the viharn built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a> lies in the posture of a reclining lion and represents the Buddha's passing into Nirvana. It is 15 m high and 46 m long, made of a brick core modelled in plaster and gilded, and the soles of its feet, inlaid with mother-of-pearl, are divided into 108 panels showing auspicious symbols.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Wat Pho was meant as a place of public education. A pictorial encyclopedia was engraved on granite slabs on history, medicine, health, custom, literature, proverbs, lexicography and the Buddhist religion, with texts written by about 50 people from the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a> and learned monks led by Supreme Patriarch Prince Paramanuchitchinorot. UNESCO registered the marble illustrations and inscriptions in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme">Memory of the World Programme</a> on 21 February 2008. Wat Pho is regarded as the first university of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Plaques show the pressure points used in traditional Thai massage, the temple still has a school of Thai medicine, and Thai massage has been included in UNESCO's list of Intangible Cultural Heritage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wat Pho” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culturally the early kings rebuilt Ayutthayan court culture: the Ramakien, the Thai version of the Ramayana, was compiled under Rama I, the poet Sunthorn Phu composed Phra Aphai Mani in the early nineteenth century, and temples such as <b>Wat Pho</b> and Wat Arun gave Bangkok its skyline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ban Chiang National Museum presents the site, its discovery and its interpretation, with an open excavation pit at the temple of <b>Wat Pho</b> Si Mai, some 700 metres away, showing artefacts and replica skeletons as they were found.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ban-chiang-culture">Ban Chiang culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
